  <desc>Change the subject and message that invoices are emailed
  with.</desc>
    <credit type="author">
      <name>Reuben Rissler</name>
      <email>pygtk.posting@gmail.com</email>
    </credit>
  </info>

  <title>Default email message</title>

  <p>When an invoice is posted with <gui>Email invoice</gui> checked, the
  <link xref="send_email"/> window opens with this subject and message
  already filled in. It can still be changed there before it is sent; this
  window sets what every invoice starts with. Open it with <gui>Invoice
  message...</gui> in <link xref="settings#mail_accounts"/>.</p>

  <p>Type the <gui>Subject</gui> and the message. These words are replaced
  for each invoice, as listed under <gui>Filled in for each
  invoice</gui>:</p>
  <terms>
    <item><title><code>{customer}</code></title>
    <p>The name of the customer it is going to.</p></item>
    <item><title><code>{invoice}</code></title>
    <p>The invoice number.</p></item>
    <item><title><code>{total}</code></title>
    <p>The amount, such as $1,234.56.</p></item>
    <item><title><code>{company}</code></title>
    <p>Your company's name.</p></item>
  </terms>
  <p>The line under that list shows the subject as a real invoice would
  carry it. A
  word in braces that is not one of these, such as a misspelt
  <code>{custmer}</code>, is sent exactly as typed, and the preview shows it
  that way so it can be caught here.</p>

  <p>Click <gui>Save</gui> to keep the message for every computer.
  <gui>Restore the standard message</gui> puts back the wording PyGtk
  Posting started with; it is not kept until you click <gui>Save</gui>.</p>

  <note><p>Only invoices use this message. Documents and the unpaid invoices
  summary are sent with a fixed message, which can be changed in the <link
  xref="send_email"/> window before sending.</p></note>

</page>
